  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Milnesville originated as a coal mining town in the anthracite region, named after William Milnes who opened the colliery around 1850.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milnesville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The community grew rapidly with a Methodist church, company store, and post office by the late 19th century, William Monroe as postmaster.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milnesville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Milnesville sits at 1,608 feet elevation, coordinates 40°59′25″N 75°58′58″W.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milnesville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Winters drop to about 18°F, with roughly 47 inches of snow each year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milnesville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Jeddo Tunnel discharge from nearby mines has lowered stream pH to 4.3, raising iron, manganese, aluminum levels, impacting water quality.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/milnesville_pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The area’s mixed hardwood forests host species like white‑tailed deer, black bears, and the state‑endangered northern flying squirrel.",
